Silo patch 0015 — ntdll: IPv4 socket options on an IPv6 socket fall back to their IPv6 counterpart

Origin: Silo, written from scratch for this source (2026-11-20). The idea comes from MacGameFix's patches
0026/0027 (prsloan, for Forza Horizon 6's online play); no code was taken, since that project is GPL-3.0.
License: LGPL-2.1-or-later, like Wine.

Why Silo carries it: online play. Windows applies the IPPROTO_IP options of an AF_INET6 socket to the IPv4
traffic it carries (a dual-stack socket's IPv4-mapped peers); macOS rejects them there with EINVAL, so the
program sees WSAEINVAL. Forza Horizon 6 binds its UDP game socket to [::]:3074 with IPV6_V6ONLY off, sets
IP_DONTFRAGMENT and IP_TTL, and closes the socket when either fails, so matchmaking never joins a session; any
game that does the same is hit. Where the host refuses one of IP_TTL, IP_TOS, IP_DONTFRAG, IP_MULTICAST_TTL or
IP_MULTICAST_LOOP on an AF_INET6 socket (set or get), the IPv6 option that does the same job is used instead.
A host that accepts the IPv4 option never takes this path, so plain IPv4 sockets are unchanged.
Measured 2026-11-20 with a Winsock probe on a dual-stack UDP socket: without the patch all 11 set/get calls
return WSAEINVAL; with it all succeed, and a datagram sent to ::ffff:127.0.0.1 after IP_TTL 77 reaches a
native IPv4 receiver with TTL 77. The same probe on an AF_INET socket gives identical results before and after.
dlls/ntdll/unix/socket.c only.

